# modules.cpp inertial measurement interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.1 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented portable inertial measurement interface and its QMI8658 controller
driver. mm.imu, the QMI8658 driver, the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board provider, and
the board-smoke application exist. Visual sensor behavior on hardware remains
a hardware-qualification requirement rather than a completed claim.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented mm.mcu
interface, including the I2C transport this driver uses. docs/modules-platforms.mdy
remains authoritative for SDK and board selection. docs/modules-mm.mdy remains
authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

# Decision

Inertial measurement is a device service layered above mm.mcu I2C. It is not
a new transport, a new group of virtual functions on mm::mcu::Platform, and
not a policy module that fuses, filters, or calibrates.

The intended dependency direction is:

Opening fence
portable application
        |
     mm.imu
        |
selected board IMU provider
        |
mm.imu.qmi8658 (or another controller)
        |
I2C from mm.mcu
        |
selected MCU platform provider
Closing fence

An application using only portable inertial behavior declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.imu
Closing fence

It does not name a controller, vendor, I2C address, or provider module. The
selected physical board profile supplies those facts.

# Portable interface

mm.imu is a platform interface with an allocation-free API for scale,
initialization, atomic axis readings, temperature, and sleep. Its fallback
object reports Unsupported.

The public shape is:

Opening fence
enum class Status { Ok, BadArgument, Unsupported,
                    NotInitialized, Busy, Timeout, TransportError };

struct Axes {
    int x = 0;
    int y = 0;
    int z = 0;
};

struct Scale {
    unsigned int acceleration_range_g = 0;
    unsigned int rotation_range_dps = 0;
    unsigned int full_scale = 0;
};

class Imu {
public:
    virtual ~Imu() = default;

    [[nodiscard]] virtual Scale scale() const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status read(Axes& acceleration, Axes& rotation);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status temperature(int& centidegrees);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status sleep();
};

void set_imu(Imu&);
[[nodiscard]] Imu& selected_imu();
Closing fence

Readings are signed counts in the sensor's own axes. What a count is worth is
Scale's business: an acceleration count is
acceleration_range_g / full_scale of a g; a rotation count is
rotation_range_dps / full_scale of a degree per second. A caller converts to
whatever units it wants, and no caller pays for units it does not. Doing the
conversion in this module would force floating point on every executable that
reaches mm.imu, including bare-metal lanes with no hardware, to produce one
of the several unit systems callers actually want. The reference drivers this
interface was modelled on make that choice with a compile-time switch, which
is evidence that the choice belongs to the caller.

`read` fills both axis sets from one sensor reading so a caller cannot pair
an acceleration with a rotation the sensor measured at a different moment.
`temperature` reports hundredths of a degree Celsius, the sensor's precision
expressed without a floating-point type. The out-parameter is written only
when the call answers Ok.

The data-ready interrupt a sensor may offer is deliberately absent, as it is
in mm.touch and mm.rtc: a caller polls read, and mm.mcu has no callback
registration. A provider may use its own interrupt internally to make that
cheap without exposing it.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board IMU provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.imu.

# Controller driver

mm.imu.qmi8658 implements the QST QMI8658 six-axis inertial sensor over
portable mm.mcu I2C. It contains no vendor header and no board pin number: a
board's provider supplies the wiring and this module drives the protocol.

The driver receives immutable wiring and configuration descriptors:

Opening fence
struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::I2cConfiguration i2c;
    unsigned int address;          // 0x6b
    unsigned int alternate_address; // 0x6a
};

enum class AccelerationRange { G2, G4, G8, G16 };
enum class RotationRange { Dps32, Dps64, Dps128, Dps256, Dps512, Dps1024,
                           Dps2048, Dps4096 };
enum class OutputRate { Hz8000, Hz4000, Hz2000, Hz1000, Hz500,
                       Hz250, Hz125, Hz62_5, Hz31_25 };

struct Configuration {
    AccelerationRange acceleration;
    RotationRange rotation;
    OutputRate acceleration_rate;
    OutputRate rotation_rate;
};
Closing fence

The part answers at one of two addresses depending on how its SA0 pin is
strapped. The driver probes both and records which one replied, so a provider
that does not know the strap still works.

Registers are eight-bit, so a read is one address byte followed by the reply.
Every register access is an mm.mcu `i2c_write_read` rather than a write and a
read, because the sensor's address pointer must not be exposed to another
master between the two halves.

The driver owns a state machine distinguishing Idle, Ready, and Sleeping.
Initialization configures I2C, identifies the part at either address, applies
the configured ranges and output rates, and reaches Ready. Sleep puts the
sensor in its low-power state; a subsequent read requires reinitialization.

Three things in the Waveshare reference driver for the RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8
are deliberately not reproduced. Its unit conversion is absent, because
mm.imu reports counts and a scale and lets the caller choose units. Its
AttitudeEngine, magnetometer, and wake-on-motion paths are absent, because the
board wires none of them and a driver should not carry registers nobody can
reach. Its address retry loop is kept, because the part answers at one of two
addresses and which one is a board fact the driver can discover rather than
demand.

# Provider binding

mm.imu carries the existing platform-interface marker. A concrete hardware
board binds it to one unique provider:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.imu platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.imu
Closing fence

The provider imports mm.imu, the selected controller module, and mm.mcu. It
owns the fixed I2C wiring and sensor address, constructs the controller, and
registers the Imu implementation from a retained static object. Existing
provider validation, board-over-SDK precedence, conditional closure injection,
and core platform-edge rules apply unchanged.

# Test strategy

Controller tests use a fake MCU Platform that records I2C operations. Tests
compare semantic register transcripts rather than merely checking that a
source file compiled.

Required host tests cover:

- address discovery at both strapped addresses;
- initialization transcript for the exact part revision;
- configured ranges and output rates appear in the correct registers;
- atomic read returns both axis sets from one transaction;
- temperature conversion to hundredths of a degree;
- sleep and the reinitialization path;
- transport errors propagated without continuing the register sequence;
- no IMU or provider objects in an unrelated executable closure.

Target qualification builds one portable app through the real Pico external
CMake bridge, validates the ELF and UF2, and confirms exactly one IMU and MCU
provider registration object. It then flashes through flash.sh.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add mm.mcu:i2c with host transcript tests.
2. Implemented: add mm.imu with a host stand-in independent of any controller.
3. Implemented: add mm.imu.qmi8658 with initialization, read, and sleep tests.
4. Implemented: add the rp2350_touch_lcd_28 board IMU provider.
5. Implemented: add apps/board-smoke using mm.imu alongside mm.display,
   mm.touch, and mm.rtc.
6. Pending hardware: qualify readings, ranges, temperature, and sleep on the
   named Waveshare board.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.imu links with exactly one selected IMU provider
  and the provider's controller closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no IMU binding.
- An app not importing mm.imu contains no IMU provider or controller
  registration object.
- Controller host tests pass without Pico SDK or physical hardware.
- I2C register accesses cross the platform boundary as write-read
  transactions, not separate write and read calls.
- No controller source includes vendor or Pico SDK headers.
- A successful link or flash is not reported as a successful sensor reading.

# What this does not settle

- QMI8658 is the first implemented controller. Its magnetometer,
  attitude engine, and wake-on-motion paths are absent because the board wires
  none of them.
- Orientation fusion, filtering, gesture detection, and calibration are policy
  above this module.
- A data-ready interrupt is not exposed; mm.mcu has no callback registration.
- Multi-sensor selection and runtime sensor discovery are not specified.
- Temperature beyond the sensor's hundredths-of-a-degree precision is not
  promised.
